  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1113 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday...\n\nA few post-frontal showers and storms fizzled across far southeast \nNebraska this evening, as temperatures fell into the upper 50s and \nlow 60s. Overnight, lows will continue to fall into the low 50s and \n60s. \n\nA subtle shortwave will ripple through the upper level flow early \nSunday morning. Warm moist air will advect back into the region \nearly tomorrow, allowing a line of showers and storms to develop. \nWhile a stronger storm or two may be possible, widespread severe \nstorms look unlikely, given the environment characterized by \nrelatively strong deep layer shear, but little to no instability. \nForecast soundings depict PWATs up to 1.4\", which is in the top 10% \nfor this time of year. Thankfully, less intense updrafts and \nscattered nature of storms should help mitigate flooding concerns, \nalthough a few pockets of heavy rainfall will be possible. Storms \nshould diminish in coverage through the afternoon hours, though \nclouds will likely linger through the majority of the day. Expect \nafternoon highs to remain cool, in the lower 70s. \n\nMonday...\n\nStronger warm air advection ramps up Monday, as a shallow ridge \ndevelops ahead of an approaching cold front. Highs on Monday will \nbump up into the mid 80s to lower 90s, despite the development \nof scattered showers in the warm/moist airmass. \n\nThe previously mentioned cold front will swing through the region \nlate Monday afternoon and evening, sparking off another round of \nshowers and storms. 40-50 kts of 0-6 km shear, and decent \ninstability will allow initial storms to become strong to severe, \ncapable of producing large hail and damaging winds. Additionally, \nnotable turning in low level hodographs suggests a tornado or two \nwon't be out of the question. Heavy rainfall could also lead \nlocalized flash flooding, where storms manage to train over the same \narea. \n\nStorm coverage will increase through the overnight hours, but severe \nparameters will gradually wane after sunset, as the environment \nbecomes more worked over. \n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nThe frontal boundary will linger from western Kansas through \nsouthern Iowa through much of the upcoming week, keeping us cool and \nwet. Multiple rounds of showers and storms can be expected, \nespecially over the southern half of the forecast area. While \ndetails will greatly depend on the exact location of the boundary, \nan occasional strong storm or two may be possible, as is lightly \nhighlighted by Machine Learning probs, especially Tuesday through \nWednesday. Overall rainfall amounts will likely be above normal for \nthis time of yea, given the persistent signal through next week. \nHowever, the scattered storm mode and drawn out nature of the events \nshould help mitigate overall flooding potential. \n\nTemperatures will remain nice and cool through the upcoming week. \nHighs will generally top out in the 70s, with overnight lows falling \ninto the 50s and 60s.",
